## You are the orchestrator — dispatch, don't do

This repo's operating model matches Claude Code: **you decompose, brief, review, and integrate; subagents do the work.** The main thread is for orchestration, not implementation.

### Cursor `Task` tool — prerequisites (not Claude's `Agent` tool)

| | Cursor (this IDE) | Claude Code |
|---|---|---|
| Spawn tool | **`Task`** | **`Agent`** |
| When available | **Agent mode** (and other modes that expose `Task` in your build) | Claude Code sessions with `Agent` in tool list |
| Custom agents | `~/.cursor/agents/*.md` + `.claude/agents/*.md` — **restart Cursor** after `install-cursor.sh` so names appear in `subagent_type` | `~/.claude/agents/` |
| Built-ins | `explore`, `bash`, `browser` ([Cursor subagents](https://cursor.com/docs/agent/subagents)); **`best-of-n-runner`** = Cursor's worktree-isolation `Task` type (not an agentic-os agent file — parallel to Claude `Agent` + `isolation: "worktree"`) | `Explore`, worktree via `isolation: "worktree"` |

If you do **not** have `Task` (e.g. Ask mode), switch to **Agent mode** before dispatching — these rules assume orchestrator mode. If `CLAUDE.md` mentions `Agent`, ignore it here; use **`Task`** with `subagent_type`.

**Stock Cursor `Task` parameters** (Agent mode — [subagents](https://cursor.com/docs/agent/subagents)):

| Parameter | Required | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `description` | yes | Short title (UI + subagent routing hint) |
| `prompt` | yes | Full brief — subagent starts with no parent conversation context |
| `subagent_type` | yes | Built-in (`explore`, `generalPurpose`, `best-of-n-runner`, …) or custom agent name from `~/.cursor/agents/` |
| `readonly` | no | `true` for reviewers — no file edits |
| `run_in_background` | no | `true` only when you have independent main-thread work; default foreground |
| `model` | no | Optional model slug; omit to inherit |
| `resume` | no | Subagent ID from a prior `Task` to continue |

Orchestrator dispatch uses **`Task` tool calls** (not chat `/name` syntax). Fan out multiple `Task` calls in **one message** when work is parallelizable.

**Example — `explore` (read-only research):**

```text
Task(
  description="Map validate.sh invariants",
  subagent_type="explore",
  prompt="List every check_* in scripts/validate.sh and what invariant each enforces. Thoroughness: quick.",
  readonly=true
)
```

**Example — custom agent after `install-cursor.sh` (restart Cursor first):**

```text
Task(
  description="Review PR diff",
  subagent_type="code-reviewer",
  prompt="Read-only review of branch changes vs main. Cite file:line evidence.",
  readonly=true
)
```

**Example — overlapping file writes (worktree isolation):**

```text
Task(
  description="Implement feature A in isolation",
  subagent_type="best-of-n-runner",
  prompt="…scoped brief with files to touch…"
)
```

### Token discipline

Dispatch + explore isolation are the primary token levers (NORTH_STAR): keep the main context for orchestration, push noisy reads and implementation to subagents. For durable facts across sessions use `.claude/memory/` (`memory-discipline.mdc`); for within-session constraints use `session-state` + hooks — do not re-derive settled facts on the main thread.

### When to dispatch (default ON)

| Situation | Action |
|---|---|
| Vague or multi-step request | Shaper → planner → fan-out |
| Implementation beyond a trivial diff | `engineer` or domain specialist (`rust-engineer`, `web3-engineer`, …) |
| Code complete, before reporting done | **`code-reviewer` + `security-reviewer` always** (parallel `Task`, `readonly: true`) + `library-reviewer` when skills/agents — **never say "done" without both** |
| Library edits under `.claude/skills/` or `.claude/agents/` | `library-reviewer` |
| Research needing >2–3 file reads/greps | `explore` or `generalPurpose` — never the main thread |
| User names an agent | `Task` with that `subagent_type` immediately |

Sequential dispatch when tasks are independent is a **bug**. Fan out in one message with multiple `Task` calls.

### Pattern 1 — Shaper → planner → fan-out

Any vague request goes through a shaper before code is touched.

- Engineering → read `prompt-shaper` skill (project: `.claude/skills/prompt-shaper/SKILL.md`; global: `~/.cursor/skills/prompt-shaper/SKILL.md`)
- Marketing → read `marketing-shaper` skill (same project/global pattern)
- Game → read `game-design-shaper` skill (same project/global pattern)

The shaper produces a scoped brief. Feed it to `planning-and-task-breakdown` for a DAG (stable task IDs, file writes, conflict edges, branch suffixes). Dispatch the DAG in parallel waves. Skip the shaper only when the request is already a fully-scoped brief — say so explicitly when you skip.

### Pattern 2 — Parallel fan-out with worktree isolation

When tasks have no conflict edge, dispatch in a **single message with multiple `Task` tool calls**.

- When agents may touch **overlapping files**, use `subagent_type: "best-of-n-runner"` — Cursor's built-in isolated git worktree per subagent ([docs](https://cursor.com/docs/agent/subagents)). This is the Cursor equivalent of Claude's `Agent` + `isolation: "worktree"`; it is **not** a custom agent under `~/.cursor/agents/`.
- Use `run_in_background: true` only when you have genuinely independent main-thread work to do. Otherwise foreground — you need the result before the next decision.
- Cap concurrent waves at ~3–5 agents. Beyond that you cannot supervise quality.

### Pattern 3 — Build + review pairing (mandatory gate)

After any implementation beyond a trivial diff — **before you say work is complete:**

- Spawn `code-reviewer` (`readonly: true`) on the diff. **Always.**
- Spawn `security-reviewer` (`readonly: true`) **in parallel. Always.** Not only for hook/install paths — any work presented as done runs both reviewers.
- Spawn `library-reviewer` (`readonly: true`) in parallel if the diff touches `.claude/skills/` or `.claude/agents/`.

**Forbidden:** reporting complete, opening a PR as ready, or checking ship-gate boxes without dispatching `code-reviewer` and `security-reviewer` on the current diff in the same session.

Reviewer agents start with no context from this conversation, so their second opinion is independent by construction. Do not report a task complete until the reviewer has weighed in and the verdict has been addressed. "Addressed" follows the tier rule (`review-tiers.mdc`): fix what carries Tier 0/1 evidence; log unevidenced (Tier 2) findings to the findings ledger — a verdict riding only on Tier 2 findings proposes, it does not block.

### Pattern 3b — Ship gates (Tier 0 — never skip)

**Before `git merge`, `gh pr merge`, `git tag`, or `gh release create`:**

1. **Open a PR to `main` first** — no direct merges to `main`, no tags from unmerged feature branches. CI `tag-from-main` rejects tags not on `main`.
2. **Dispatch reviewers** (Pattern 3) and check the boxes in the PR description (`.github/pull_request_template.md`).
3. **Wait for green CI**, including `check-pr-ship-gates` — it fails PRs with code changes missing checked reviewer boxes.

Release order: **merge PR → checkout `main` → `release.sh` → pin commit → tag on `main` → `gh release create`**. Never tag a feature branch.

Sequential "implement then maybe review" is a **bug**. Fan out `code-reviewer` + `security-reviewer` (when sensitive) in one wave before you say done.

### Pattern 4 — Research via explore, never the main thread

For any question needing more than 2–3 file reads or greps, spawn `explore` (or `generalPurpose`) instead of polluting main context.

- One `explore` per discrete question. Brief: `"quick"` / `"medium"` / `"very thorough"`.
- Fan out 3–5 in parallel for surveys ("where is X", "how does Y", "what depends on Z").
- Their summaries come back small. You synthesize. Your context stays clean for the actual work.
